The Python binding gives you `Session` and `Host` with native Python values over
`ctypes`
([`pancake`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/bindings/python/pancake)).
The transport modules are `pancake.tcp`, `pancake.inproc`, and lazy
`pancake.iroh`. The package is not published yet; start from the
[checkout instructions]($link.page('bindings')).

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, send,
handle the reply) reads like this in Python. `serve` runs the inbound loop as a
background task, and `fetch` and `send` start it on demand, so a pure caller
can skip the explicit call.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```python
> from pancake import connect, tcp_locator
>
> # Plain TCP uses a cleartext host and port.
> partner = tcp_locator("127.0.0.1", 9099)
>
> session = await connect(partner)
> session.serve()
>
> greeter = await session.fetch("greeter")  # a capability, by name
> try:
>     reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"])
>     print(reply)
> except Exception as reason:
>     print(f"the greeter refused: {reason}")
>
> await session.close()
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```python
> from pancake import connect
> from pancake.iroh import Reachability
>
> # DISCOVERY resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh"
>
> session = await connect(partner, Reachability.DISCOVERY)
> session.serve()
>
> greeter = await session.fetch("greeter")  # a capability, by name
> try:
>     reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"])
>     print(reply)
> except Exception as reason:
>     print(f"the greeter refused: {reason}")
>
> await session.close()
> ```

The iroh tab needs the optional transport library and a discovery-reachable
node id. The session calls stay the same.

Hosting works from Python too: `listen` binds a port and accepts sessions that
publish and serve, and `listen_keyed_tcp` is the restart-stable keyed flavor.

## Async

Everything rides plain
[`asyncio`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html).
`await session.send(...)` settles with the reply, and `session.serve()` runs
the inbound loop as a background task until the session closes.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the CapTP ideas.

## Actors

`session.spawn` and `session.publish` take a `behavior(verb, args)` callable. A
raise breaks the answer with the exception's own words, and each deliver
crosses into Python once, through one trampoline.

The full surface lives in the
[Python binding README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/bindings/python/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/apps/rooms/clients/python/room_client.py)
shows the rest.
